D("portraiture",{etymology:E("From portrait + -ure, through French; portrait ultimately comes from Latin protrahere ‘draw forth’. Portraiture names the art or practice of creating portraits and a style of depicting a subject.",null,"portrait","-ure","Etymonline, portrait (https://www.etymonline.com/word/portrait)."),contexts:[C("portraiture.s1","藝術","The exhibition traced portraiture from formal royal canvases to candid photographs of ordinary workers.","展覽從正式的王室畫像追溯到普通工人的自然攝影，呈現肖像藝術的演變。","Portraiture is the creation of portraits or a particular style of depicting a subject.","Portraiture is the object of traced; from and to mark the range.","The exhibition studies the art and changing styles of portraying people.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The course on portraiture excluded every image of a person and focused only on diagrams of machines.","The course on portraiture studied how artists create images of people.","Portraiture is the art or practice of making portraits, not machine diagrams alone.")],quiz:[Q("portraiture.q1","portraiture.s1","meaning","Portraiture is the art of ___.",["creating portraits","repairing computer ports","measuring rainfall"],"creating portraits","The noun describes the practice of depicting people.","meaning"),Q("portraiture.q2","portraiture.s1","context","A book comparing painted royal likenesses and modern headshots studies ___.",["portraiture","polymerization","portage"],"portraiture","Both kinds of likeness are examples of portrait art.","context"),Q("portraiture.q3","portraiture.s1","word-choice","The museum’s new gallery explores the history of ____ across cultures.",["portraiture","pork","porosity"],"portraiture","The gallery examines styles and practices of making portraits.","word-choice"),Q("portraiture.q4","portraiture.s1","meaning","What might a “style of portraiture” describe?",["A way artists depict a person","A way ships enter a harbor","A way food is divided into servings"],"A way artists depict a person","Portraiture includes a particular approach to portraying a subject.","meaning")]});
D("portray",{etymology:E("From French portraire ‘depict, draw’, from Latin protrahere ‘draw forth’; the verb means represent someone or something in a particular way, or act a character’s part.","pro","trahere","-","Etymonline, portray (https://www.etymonline.com/word/portray)."),contexts:[C("portray.s1","藝術","The documentary portrays the fishing village as resilient, showing residents rebuilding after each storm.","紀錄片把漁村描繪成堅韌的社區，呈現居民如何在每場風暴後重建。","To portray is to describe or show somebody or something in a particular way.","Portrays takes village as object; as resilient gives the representation.","The film presents the community through its recovery and persistence."),C("portray.s2","戲劇","An actor will portray the young astronomer in the stage adaptation of her diary.","一位演員將在日記改編的舞台劇中飾演年輕的天文學家。","To portray can mean act the part of a character.","Portray takes astronomer as object; in the adaptation identifies the production.","The actor performs the role of a specific person.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The film portrayed the village as peaceful by showing no residents, no homes, and no evidence that anyone lived there.","The film portrayed the village as peaceful by showing families sharing the square after work.","Portray means represent or show a subject in a particular way; the first scene gives no village life to support the description.")],quiz:[Q("portray.q1","portray.s1","meaning","To portray a town as thriving is to ___.",["represent it as successful and active","erase it from every image","measure its harbor"],"represent it as successful and active","The verb means show or describe a subject in a particular way.","meaning"),Q("portray.q2","portray.s2","context","Who portrays Hamlet in a play?",["The actor playing Hamlet","The audience member buying a ticket","The stage curtain"],"The actor playing Hamlet","The role is acted by the performer.","context"),Q("portray.q3","portray.s1","word-choice","The article tried to ____ the expedition as a carefully planned success.",["portray","possess","pore"],"portray","The article represents the expedition in a specific light.","word-choice"),Q("portray.q4","portray.s2","meaning","What does an actor do when portraying a historical figure?",["Acts the part of that person","Paints the theater’s entrance","Carries a canoe over land"],"Acts the part of that person","This is the theatrical sense of portray.","meaning")]});
D("portrayal",{etymology:E("From portray + -al, with -al forming a noun for an act or result; portray derives through French from Latin protrahere ‘draw forth’. A portrayal is a representation in art, writing, or performance.",null,"portray","-al","Etymonline, portray (https://www.etymonline.com/word/portray)."),contexts:[C("portrayal.s1","通用","The film’s portrayal of the nurse emphasized her skill and humor rather than reducing her to a side character.","電影對護士的刻畫強調她的專業和幽默，而非把她簡化成配角。","A portrayal is a representation of a person or scene in art, writing, or performance.","Portrayal is the subject; of the nurse identifies its focus.","The film’s representation gives the character depth and agency.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The film’s portrayal of the nurse was the nurse herself, physically present as the actor and not a representation at all.","The film’s portrayal of the nurse was the way the character was represented on screen.","A portrayal is a representation or depiction, not the real person being represented.")],quiz:[Q("portrayal.q1","portrayal.s1","meaning","A portrayal is ___.",["a representation of a person or scene","a container for water","a system of pipes"],"a representation of a person or scene","The noun refers to how a subject is shown in a work.","meaning"),Q("portrayal.q2","portrayal.s1","context","A critic discusses an actor’s portrayal of a queen. The critic studies ___.",["how the actor represents the character","the queen’s actual crown material","the theater’s ticket price"],"how the actor represents the character","Portrayal describes the performance’s representation of the role.","context"),Q("portrayal.q3","portrayal.s1","word-choice","The novel’s ____ of rural life was praised for its detail.",["portrayal","portage","pore"],"portrayal","The novel presents a representation of rural life.","word-choice"),Q("portrayal.q4","portrayal.s1","meaning","Why might two books offer different portrayals of the same leader?",["They may represent the person in different ways.","They use different liquid measures.","They contain the same photograph by definition."],"They may represent the person in different ways.","A portrayal is a way of depicting a subject.","meaning")]});
D("pose",{etymology:E("From French poser ‘put, place’, from Latin pausare ‘pause, set down’; the verb extended from placing the body for an image to creating a problem or pretending, while the noun names a posture.",null,"pausare",null,"Etymonline, pose (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pose)."),contexts:[C("pose.s1","藝術","The model posed beside the window while the photographer adjusted the reflector.","模特兒在窗邊擺好姿勢，攝影師則調整反光板。","To pose is to sit or stand in a particular position for a photograph or painting.","Posed beside gives the model’s position; while marks the simultaneous adjustment.","The model holds a position so the photographer can make an image."),C("pose.s2","書面","The cracked gas line poses a serious danger to residents in the apartment building.","破裂的瓦斯管線對公寓居民構成嚴重危險。","To pose can mean create a problem, danger, or question that must be dealt with.","Poses takes danger as object; to residents identifies those at risk.","The damaged line creates a safety risk that requires action."),C("pose.s3","通用","The caller posed as a bank employee and asked the customer to reveal a password.","來電者冒充銀行職員，要求顧客透露密碼。","To pose as someone is to pretend to be that person.","Posed as introduces the false identity; and links the request.","The caller falsely claims an identity to deceive the customer."),C("pose.s4","通用","The dancer held a dramatic pose at the end of the routine while the lights faded.","舞者在舞步結尾保持戲劇性的姿態，燈光逐漸暗下。","A pose is the position in which someone stands or sits, especially for effect.","Pose is the object of held; at the end specifies its timing.","The dancer deliberately holds a shaped body position.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The scammer posed as a bank employee by honestly telling customers he was a stranger with no bank connection.","The scammer posed as a bank employee by falsely claiming to represent the bank.","To pose as someone means pretend to be them; honestly denying the identity is not impersonation.")],quiz:[Q("pose.q1","pose.s1","meaning","A model poses for a photograph by ___.",["holding a chosen body position","sending a payment","building a harbor"],"holding a chosen body position","This is the verb sense of adopting a posture for an image.","meaning"),Q("pose.q2","pose.s2","context","A loose wire poses a danger because it ___.",["creates a risk that must be addressed","pretends to be an electrician","holds a photographic posture"],"creates a risk that must be addressed","The verb means create a problem or danger.","context"),Q("pose.q3","pose.s3","meaning","If someone poses as a doctor, they ___.",["pretend to be a doctor","sit for a medical photograph only","write a prescription legally"],"pretend to be a doctor","Pose as means falsely claim an identity.","meaning"),Q("pose.q4","pose.s4","word-choice","The statue’s ____ showed a runner leaning toward the finish line.",["pose","possessor","possibility"],"pose","The sculpted body position is its pose.","word-choice")]});
D("posh",{etymology:E("Posh is British informal English for smart, expensive, or upper-class in style. Its origin is uncertain; the popular expansion ‘port out, starboard home’ is unsupported and should not be treated as fact.",null,null,null,"Etymonline, posh (https://www.etymonline.com/word/posh)."),contexts:[C("posh.s1","外貌","They booked a posh hotel with marble floors, a rooftop pool, and a doorman in a tailored uniform.","他們訂了一家高檔飯店，鋪著大理石地板，有頂樓泳池和穿訂制服的門房。","Posh means smart and expensive in a way associated with upper-class style.","Posh modifies hotel; with lists details that suggest luxury.","The facilities and service make the hotel feel expensive and upscale.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The posh hotel was a bare tent with no furnishings, services, or expensive features of any kind.","The posh hotel was luxurious and expensive, with elegant rooms and attentive service.","Posh describes an upscale, smart, expensive style, not a bare campsite.")],quiz:[Q("posh.q1","posh.s1","meaning","A posh restaurant is likely ___.",["expensive and stylish","run-down and very cheap","located only underwater"],"expensive and stylish","Posh describes an upscale, smart style.","meaning"),Q("posh.q2","posh.s1","context","Which place seems posh?",["A hotel with marble halls and a formal doorman","A broken shed with no roof","A simple bench beside a pond"],"A hotel with marble halls and a formal doorman","Those details suggest a luxurious establishment.","context"),Q("posh.q3","posh.s1","word-choice","They wore their best clothes for dinner at the ____ club.",["posh","porous","polyp"],"posh","The club is described as elegant and high-end.","word-choice"),Q("posh.q4","posh.s1","meaning","Which claim about the origin of posh is safest?",["Its origin is uncertain; the ‘port out’ expansion is unsupported.","It definitely comes from a nautical acronym.","It is Latin for swimming pool."],"Its origin is uncertain; the ‘port out’ expansion is unsupported.","Etymological claims should reflect the uncertain evidence.","meaning")]});
D("posit",{etymology:E("From Latin positus, past participle of ponere ‘place, set’; English posit means put forward or assume a proposition as true for the sake of argument or explanation.",null,"ponere","-","Etymonline, posit (https://www.etymonline.com/word/posit)."),contexts:[C("posit.s1","學術","The model posits that the two species shared an ancestor before their habitats separated.","模型假設兩個物種在棲地分化前曾有共同祖先。","To posit is to suggest or assume something as true for discussion.","Posits takes that-clause as object; before establishes the proposed sequence.","The model puts forward an assumption that can be examined with evidence.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The researcher posited the theory by proving it beyond doubt before proposing or assuming anything for discussion.","The researcher posited the theory as a hypothesis to be tested against evidence.","Posit means put forward an assumption or proposition, not necessarily prove it conclusively.")],quiz:[Q("posit.q1","posit.s1","meaning","To posit an explanation is to ___.",["propose it as an assumption for discussion","prove it beyond all possible doubt","hide it from every reader"],"propose it as an assumption for discussion","Posit means put forward or assume an idea.","meaning"),Q("posit.q2","posit.s1","context","A scientist posits that a chemical causes the reaction. The scientist ___.",["proposes that idea for testing","has already disproved it","is describing a job title"],"proposes that idea for testing","The claim is an assumption to evaluate with evidence.","context"),Q("posit.q3","posit.s1","word-choice","For the sake of the debate, let us ____ that both routes remain open.",["posit","possess","portray"],"posit","The speaker proposes an assumption for discussion.","word-choice"),Q("posit.q4","posit.s1","meaning","Why might a researcher posit a hypothesis before an experiment?",["To state a proposition that evidence can later test.","To report a result already measured as certain.","To divide a meal into servings."],"To state a proposition that evidence can later test.","Positing establishes a proposed claim for investigation.","meaning")]});
D("position",{etymology:E("From Latin positio ‘placing, position’, from positus, past participle of ponere ‘put, place’; English developed senses for location, job, opinion, and the act of putting something somewhere.",null,"ponere","-tion","Etymonline, position (https://www.etymonline.com/word/position)."),contexts:[C("position.s1","通用","The map showed the hikers’ position on the eastern side of the ridge at noon.","地圖顯示中午時登山客位於山脊東側。","A position is the place where a person or thing is.","Position is the object of showed; on the eastern side locates it.","The noun indicates the hikers’ location at a specific time."),C("position.s2","職業","She accepted a research position at the hospital after completing her clinical training.","完成臨床訓練後，她接受了醫院的研究職位。","A position can be a job, especially a formal one.","Position is the object of accepted; at the hospital identifies the employer.","The noun refers to a paid role in an organization."),C("position.s3","通用","The committee stated its position on the proposal in a short public letter.","委員會在一封簡短公開信中表明了對提案的立場。","A position can be an opinion or attitude toward a subject.","Position is the object of stated; on the proposal specifies the issue.","The letter explains what the committee thinks about the proposal."),C("position.s4","通用","Position the fragile vase near the center of the shelf, away from the edge.","把易碎花瓶放在架子中央附近，遠離邊緣。","To position something is to put it carefully in a place.","Position is an imperative verb; near and away specify placement.","The instruction places the vase safely at a chosen spot.")],mistakes:[M("word-choice","The committee published its position on the proposal by explaining where the chairs were physically located in the room.","The committee published its position on the proposal by explaining its opinion about the plan.","Position can mean a stance or opinion; a chair’s physical location is a different sense.")],quiz:[Q("position.q1","position.s1","meaning","A marker’s position on a map is its ___.",["location","job title","attitude"],"location","This sense means where something is situated.","meaning"),Q("position.q2","position.s2","context","A person applying for a new position is applying for ___.",["a job","a map coordinate","an opinion"],"a job","Position can mean a formal job or role.","context"),Q("position.q3","position.s3","meaning","A candidate’s position on education is the candidate’s ___.",["opinion or stance","place in a parking lot","way of standing for a photo"],"opinion or stance","This sense describes an attitude toward an issue.","meaning"),Q("position.q4","position.s4","grammar","In “Position the lamp beside the sofa,” position is a ___.",["verb meaning place it","noun meaning a job","noun meaning an opinion"],"verb meaning place it","The command tells someone where to put the lamp.","grammar")]});
D("positive",{etymology:E("From Latin positivus ‘settled, definite’, from positus ‘placed’; English meanings include certain, constructive, and greater than zero or indicating a detected test result.",null,"positus","-ive","Etymonline, positive (https://www.etymonline.com/word/positive)."),contexts:[C("positive.s1","通用","I am positive the key is in the desk drawer because I put it there this morning.","我確定鑰匙在書桌抽屜裡，因為我今天早上把它放在那裡。","Positive means completely sure that something is true.","Positive is a subject complement; the because-clause gives the speaker’s evidence.","The speaker expresses certainty about the key’s location."),C("positive.s2","通用","The team kept a positive attitude after the first setback and revised the plan together.","第一次受挫後，團隊仍保持積極態度，並一起修訂計畫。","Positive can mean good, hopeful, or aimed at improvement.","Positive modifies attitude; after gives the circumstance that tests it.","The team responds hopefully and constructively rather than giving up."),C("positive.s3","科學","The patient’s test was positive for the virus, so the clinic arranged follow-up care.","病人的病毒檢測結果呈陽性，因此診所安排後續照護。","Positive can mean greater than zero or that a test found what it was looking for.","Positive is a subject complement; for names what the test detected.","A positive result means the targeted virus was detected.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The test was positive for the virus, meaning the laboratory found no trace of the virus in the sample.","The test was negative for the virus, meaning the laboratory found no trace in the sample.","A positive test indicates the target was detected; no trace corresponds to a negative result.")],quiz:[Q("positive.q1","positive.s1","meaning","If you are positive about a fact, you are ___.",["certain it is true","unsure it happened","angry about it"],"certain it is true","This sense means completely sure.","meaning"),Q("positive.q2","positive.s2","context","Which response is positive after a setback?",["The team looks for a constructive way forward.","The team gives up and refuses to try.","The team hides every result."],"The team looks for a constructive way forward.","A positive attitude is hopeful and improvement-oriented.","context"),Q("positive.q3","positive.s3","meaning","A positive medical test usually means ___.",["the test detected what it was designed to detect","the test found nothing at all","the result is always greater than a million"],"the test detected what it was designed to detect","The scientific sense indicates the target substance or condition was found.","meaning"),Q("positive.q4","positive.s2","word-choice","The coach gave the players ____ feedback that focused on what they could improve.",["positive","possessive","ponderous"],"positive","Constructive feedback is aimed at improvement.","word-choice")]});
D("positively",{etymology:E("From positive + adverb suffix -ly; positive comes from Latin positivus ‘definite, settled’. The adverb means definitely or in a constructive, approving manner.",null,"positive","-ly","Etymonline, positive (https://www.etymonline.com/word/positive)."),contexts:[C("positively.s1","通用","The director responded positively to the revised budget, approving the extra funds for safety repairs.","主管對修訂後的預算反應正面，批准增加安全維修經費。","Positively means definitely or in a constructive or approving manner.","Positively modifies responded; approving the funds explains the favorable response.","The director supports the proposal rather than rejecting it.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The director responded positively to the safety plan by rejecting every repair and criticizing the team.","The director responded negatively to the safety plan by rejecting every repair and criticizing the team.","A positive response is favorable or approving; rejection and criticism are negative.")],quiz:[Q("positively.q1","positively.s1","meaning","If a reviewer responds positively, the response is ___.",["favorable or approving","hostile and rejecting","unrelated to the review"],"favorable or approving","Positively can describe an approving response.","meaning"),Q("positively.q2","positively.s1","context","Which response is positive?",["“I support the plan and will fund the repairs.”","“I reject the plan and will block it.”","“I did not read the plan.”"],"“I support the plan and will fund the repairs.”","The response expresses clear approval and support.","context"),Q("positively.q3","positively.s1","word-choice","The interview panel spoke ____ about her experience and offered her the role.",["positively","porously","pompously"],"positively","The panel’s favorable assessment led to an offer.","word-choice"),Q("positively.q4","positively.s1","meaning","In “the medicine affected sleep positively,” positively suggests the effect was ___.",["beneficial","harmful","impossible"],"beneficial","The adverb can mean in a favorable or constructive way.","meaning")]});
D("positivism",{etymology:E("From positive + -ism, named by Auguste Comte in French positivisme; the philosophical view holds that reliable knowledge should be grounded in observable, testable facts rather than metaphysical speculation.",null,"positive","-ism","Etymonline, positivism (https://www.etymonline.com/word/positivism)."),contexts:[C("positivism.s1","哲學","The historian of science explained positivism as an approach that favors evidence from observation and experiment.","科學史學家把實證論解釋為重視觀察與實驗證據的方法。","Positivism is the view that genuine knowledge comes from observable, testable facts.","Positivism is the object of explained as; that-clause defines its principle.","The position privileges evidence that can be checked through observation or experiment.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","Positivism rejects observation and testing, claiming that reliable knowledge comes only from unverifiable metaphysical guesses.","Positivism favors observation and testing as the basis for reliable knowledge.","The core of positivism is reliance on observable, testable evidence, not rejection of it.")],quiz:[Q("positivism.q1","positivism.s1","meaning","Positivism emphasizes knowledge based on ___.",["observable and testable facts","claims that cannot be checked","dreams alone"],"observable and testable facts","The philosophical view privileges evidence open to observation and testing.","meaning"),Q("positivism.q2","positivism.s1","context","A positivist researcher is most likely to ___.",["test a claim against measurable evidence","accept a claim that cannot be examined","avoid collecting observations"],"test a claim against measurable evidence","Positivism values observable and testable facts.","context"),Q("positivism.q3","positivism.s1","word-choice","The debate contrasted ____ with explanations based only on metaphysical speculation.",["positivism","porcelain","popcorn"],"positivism","The philosophical approach grounds claims in observable evidence.","word-choice"),Q("positivism.q4","positivism.s1","meaning","What kind of evidence fits positivism?",["Results that can be observed and tested","A belief that must remain untestable","A rumor with no source"],"Results that can be observed and tested","The view limits reliable knowledge to empirically checkable claims.","meaning")]});
D("possess",{etymology:E("From Latin possidere ‘have, occupy’, from potis ‘able, in control’ + sedere ‘sit’; English uses the verb for owning property, having a quality, or being controlled by a strong feeling or spirit.","potis","sedere","-ess","Etymonline, possess (https://www.etymonline.com/word/possess)."),contexts:[C("possess.s1","書面","The family possesses a small cottage near the lake, but rents an apartment in the city for work.","這家人在湖邊擁有一間小屋，但為了工作在市區租住公寓。","To possess is to have something as property.","Possesses takes cottage as object; but contrasts it with the rented apartment.","The family owns the cottage rather than merely borrowing it."),C("possess.s2","書面","The applicant possesses unusual patience and can explain the same procedure calmly to every visitor.","申請人具有難得的耐心，能冷靜地向每位訪客重複解釋同一程序。","To possess can mean have a quality, skill, or feature.","Possesses takes patience as object; and links the quality to an example.","Patience is an attribute the applicant has."),C("possess.s3","書面","Fear possessed the child during the blackout until a familiar voice came from the hallway.","停電時恐懼攫住了孩子，直到走廊傳來熟悉的聲音。","To possess can mean take hold of a person’s mind, as a strong feeling or spirit.","Fear is the subject; possessed takes child as object; until marks the endpoint.","The feeling dominates the child’s thoughts and behavior.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The family possessed the cottage by borrowing it for one weekend without permission or ownership.","The family rented the cottage for one weekend without owning it.","Possess in the property sense means own or have as property; temporary unauthorized borrowing is not ownership.")],quiz:[Q("possess.q1","possess.s1","meaning","To possess a house is to ___.",["own or have it as property","visit it once","paint a picture of it"],"own or have it as property","The property sense means own or hold something.","meaning"),Q("possess.q2","possess.s2","context","A candidate who possesses strong leadership skills ___.",["has those abilities","has sold them","is controlled by a spirit"],"has those abilities","The verb can mean have a quality or skill.","context"),Q("possess.q3","possess.s3","meaning","If panic possesses someone, panic ___.",["takes control of their thoughts","becomes their property","moves to a new house"],"takes control of their thoughts","This literary sense means a powerful feeling dominates the person.","meaning"),Q("possess.q4","possess.s2","word-choice","The guide must ____ enough patience to answer repeated questions kindly.",["possess","portend","pore"],"possess","The guide needs to have the quality of patience.","word-choice")]});
D("possession",{etymology:E("From Latin possessio ‘occupation, ownership’, from possidere ‘possess’; the noun describes the state of owning something and, often in plural, the things owned.",null,"possidere","-ion","Etymonline, possession (https://www.etymonline.com/word/possession)."),contexts:[C("possession.s1","通用","Possession of the rare manuscript passed to the university after the collector donated it.","收藏家捐贈後，這份珍稀手稿的所有權轉移給大學。","Possession is the state of having or owning something.","Possession is the subject; of the manuscript specifies what is owned.","The sentence concerns ownership, not merely physical handling."),C("possession.s2","通用","The evacuees carried only a few possessions, including family photographs and warm clothes.","撤離者只帶了少量財物，包括家庭照片和保暖衣物。","A possession can be something that a person owns, often used in the plural.","Possessions is the object of carried; including lists examples.","The noun refers to personal belongings the people own.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The hikers packed their possessions, meaning only the borrowed equipment they did not own and left behind.","The hikers packed their possessions, meaning their own belongings such as clothes and maps.","Possessions are things someone owns; borrowed items are not their possessions in this sense.")],quiz:[Q("possession.q1","possession.s1","meaning","Legal possession of a painting concerns ___.",["the state of owning or holding it","the color of its frame","the size of the gallery"],"the state of owning or holding it","The noun names ownership or holding.","meaning"),Q("possession.q2","possession.s2","context","A traveler’s possessions might include ___.",["clothes, a passport, and a camera","a stranger’s house and car","a river’s current"],"clothes, a passport, and a camera","These are personal belongings the traveler owns.","context"),Q("possession.q3","possession.s1","word-choice","The court ruled that ____ of the land belonged to the trust.",["possession","porpoise","polyphony"],"possession","The judgment concerns who owns or holds the land.","word-choice"),Q("possession.q4","possession.s2","meaning","Why gather one’s possessions before leaving a hotel?",["They are personal belongings that one owns.","They are the hotel’s plumbing system.","They are an animal population."],"They are personal belongings that one owns.","Possessions are a person’s owned items.","meaning")]});
D("possessions",{etymology:E("Plural of possession, from Latin possessio ‘ownership’, from possidere ‘possess’; the plural commonly refers to the things owned by a person.",null,"possidere","-s","Etymonline, possession (https://www.etymonline.com/word/possession)."),contexts:[C("possessions.s1","通用","Before the evacuation, residents packed their possessions into labeled boxes and carried them to the shelter.","疏散前，居民把自己的物品裝進貼好標籤的箱子，搬到避難所。","Possessions are things that somebody owns.","Possessions is the object of packed; into gives the containers.","The residents take personal belongings with them when leaving home.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","She packed her possessions by leaving every belonging at home and carrying only items owned by strangers.","She packed her possessions by gathering the belongings she owned and taking them with her.","Possessions are one’s own belongings, not property belonging to strangers.")],quiz:[Q("possessions.q1","possessions.s1","meaning","A person’s possessions are their ___.",["belongings","unowned public roads","future possibilities"],"belongings","The plural means the things that person owns.","meaning"),Q("possessions.q2","possessions.s1","context","Which items are likely among a traveler’s possessions?",["Their suitcase and camera","A hotel’s wall and roof","A public bus route"],"Their suitcase and camera","These personal items are owned by the traveler.","context"),Q("possessions.q3","possessions.s1","word-choice","The movers wrapped the family’s ____ before loading the truck.",["possessions","positivism","portents"],"possessions","The movers are packing the family’s belongings.","word-choice"),Q("possessions.q4","possessions.s1","meaning","What does “leave your possessions behind” mean?",["Leave your owned belongings where they are.","Give everyone a job title.","Move a boat over land."],"Leave your owned belongings where they are.","Possessions are personal things someone owns.","meaning")]});
D("possessive",{etymology:E("From possess + -ive; possess comes from Latin possidere ‘own, occupy’, and -ive forms an adjective. Possessive can describe a controlling desire to own or grammar marking ownership.",null,"possess","-ive","Etymonline, possessive (https://www.etymonline.com/word/possessive)."),contexts:[C("possessive.s1","通用","The possessive partner checked every message and insisted that the other person cancel plans with friends.","佔有慾強的伴侶查看每則訊息，還堅持對方取消與朋友的約會。","Possessive describes a desire to own or control someone or something.","Possessive modifies partner; and links controlling behaviors.","The partner tries to restrict another person’s independence."),C("possessive.s2","語言學","In “her bicycle,” her is a possessive form showing who owns the bicycle.","在 her bicycle 中，her 是表示自行車所有者的所有格形式。","A possessive is a grammatical form marking possession, such as my or the student’s.","Possessive is a subject complement; showing explains its grammatical function.","The determiner her signals a relationship of ownership.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","In “their house,” their is not possessive because it identifies who owns the house.","In “their house,” their is possessive because it marks who owns the house.","Possessive forms such as their indicate ownership or a related association.")],quiz:[Q("possessive.q1","possessive.s1","meaning","A possessive friend may ___.",["try to control who the friend spends time with","encourage complete independence","avoid any personal relationship"],"try to control who the friend spends time with","The adjective describes a controlling desire to own or restrict someone.","meaning"),Q("possessive.q2","possessive.s2","context","Which word is possessive in “Mina’s coat is blue”?",["Mina’s","coat","blue"],"Mina’s","The apostrophe-s form marks who owns the coat.","context"),Q("possessive.q3","possessive.s1","word-choice","He became ____ and demanded that his partner stop seeing old friends.",["possessive","porous","positive"],"possessive","The demand shows a desire to control the partner.","word-choice"),Q("possessive.q4","possessive.s2","meaning","What does a possessive determiner such as my show?",["A relationship of ownership or association","A future event","A measure of liquid"],"A relationship of ownership or association","Possessive grammar marks whose item or relation is meant.","meaning")]});
D("possessor",{etymology:E("From Latin possessor ‘owner, occupant’, from possidere ‘possess’ + agent suffix -or; a possessor is a person who owns a thing or has a quality.",null,"possidere","-or","Etymonline, possessor (https://www.etymonline.com/word/possessor)."),contexts:[C("possessor.s1","法律","The possessor of the antique ring provided a receipt showing when and where it had been purchased.","古董戒指的持有人出示收據，證明購買時間與地點。","A possessor is someone who possesses a thing or quality.","Possessor of the ring identifies the person; provided gives their action.","The person holds or owns the ring and can document it.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The possessor of the signed painting was the person who had never owned it, held it, or had access to it.","The possessor of the signed painting was the person who owned or held it.","A possessor is someone who has or owns an item, not someone with no connection to it.")],quiz:[Q("possessor.q1","possessor.s1","meaning","A possessor of a key is someone who ___.",["has or owns the key","has never seen it","built the door only"],"has or owns the key","Possessor names the person who possesses something.","meaning"),Q("possessor.q2","possessor.s1","context","Who is the possessor of a bicycle?",["The person who owns or holds it","The person who paints a nearby wall","The person who measures the road"],"The person who owns or holds it","The possessor is the owner or holder.","context"),Q("possessor.q3","possessor.s1","word-choice","The legal ____ of the painting signed the transfer document.",["possessor","pore","portico"],"possessor","The person owning or holding the artwork signs its transfer.","word-choice"),Q("possessor.q4","possessor.s1","word-formation","What does -or contribute in possessor?",["A person who performs or has something","A place where possessions are stored","A quality meaning colorful"],"A person who performs or has something","The agent suffix forms a noun for the one who possesses.","word-formation")]});
D("possibility",{etymology:E("From possible + -ity; possible came through French from Latin possibilis, from posse ‘be able’. Possibility names the fact or chance that something may happen.",null,"posse","-ity","Etymonline, possible (https://www.etymonline.com/word/possible)."),contexts:[C("possibility.s1","通用","There is a possibility of snow overnight, so the road crew prepared salt and plows.","夜間可能下雪，因此道路工程隊準備了融雪鹽和除雪車。","Possibility is the fact that something may happen.","Possibility of snow states the uncertain event; so links it to preparation.","Snow is not certain, but there is a chance it will occur.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The possibility of rain meant that rain was absolutely impossible and no forecast was needed.","The possibility of rain meant that rain might occur, so people checked the forecast.","A possibility is something that may happen, not something impossible by definition.")],quiz:[Q("possibility.q1","possibility.s1","meaning","A possibility is something that ___.",["may happen","has already been ruled out as impossible","must always happen"],"may happen","The noun refers to a chance or potential event.","meaning"),Q("possibility.q2","possibility.s1","context","Why bring an umbrella when there is a possibility of rain?",["Rain might occur.","Rain is guaranteed never to happen.","The umbrella is a type of map."],"Rain might occur.","The forecast indicates a chance, not certainty, of rain.","context"),Q("possibility.q3","possibility.s1","word-choice","The team considered the ____ that the train would be delayed by snow.",["possibility","possession","porcelain"],"possibility","A snow delay is a potential event to consider.","word-choice"),Q("possibility.q4","possibility.s1","meaning","How does possibility differ from certainty?",["It describes something that could happen but is not assured.","It means an event is already proven.","It describes a person’s belongings."],"It describes something that could happen but is not assured.","Possibility leaves room for an event not to occur.","meaning")]});
D("possible",{etymology:E("From Old French possible, from Latin possibilis ‘that can be done’, from posse ‘be able’; the adjective describes what can exist, happen, or be done, and a candidate or cause that may be selected or true.",null,"posse","-ible","Etymonline, possible (https://www.etymonline.com/word/possible)."),contexts:[C("possible.s1","通用","It is possible to finish the repairs before winter if the replacement parts arrive this week.","如果替換零件本週到貨，就有可能在冬天前完成維修。","Possible means able to exist, happen, or be done.","Possible follows it is; to finish gives the action that can be done.","Completion depends on parts arriving, but it can happen."),C("possible.s2","通用","The detective interviewed a possible witness who had been near the station when the alarm sounded.","警探訪問了一名可能的目擊者，他在警報響起時曾在車站附近。","Possible can mean a person who might be chosen or might be the true cause or source.","Possible modifies witness; who-clause gives the reason for considering them.","The witness is a candidate, but their status is not yet confirmed.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","It was possible to reach the island only if no boat, bridge, plane, or route existed to get there.","It was impossible to reach the island because no boat, bridge, plane, or route existed.","Possible means able to happen or be done; the absence of any route makes the trip impossible.")],quiz:[Q("possible.q1","possible.s1","meaning","If a task is possible, it ___.",["can be done","cannot happen under any circumstances","has already been forgotten"],"can be done","Possible describes something able to happen or be completed.","meaning"),Q("possible.q2","possible.s2","context","A possible suspect is someone who ___.",["might have been involved but is not confirmed","has been proven innocent and excluded","is a police building"],"might have been involved but is not confirmed","Possible marks a candidate or potential cause.","context"),Q("possible.q3","possible.s1","word-choice","With two extra workers, it should be ____ to finish the wall today.",["possible","possessive","porous"],"possible","The task can be completed under that condition.","word-choice"),Q("possible.q4","possible.s2","meaning","Why call a witness possible rather than confirmed?",["The person is a candidate, but the evidence is not yet conclusive.","The person cannot exist.","The person is already convicted."],"The person is a candidate, but the evidence is not yet conclusive.","The adjective indicates a potential rather than settled identity.","meaning")]});
D("possibly",{etymology:E("From possible + adverb suffix -ly; possible comes from Latin possibilis, from posse ‘be able’. Possibly is used to say an event or claim might be true.",null,"possible","-ly","Etymonline, possible (https://www.etymonline.com/word/possible)."),contexts:[C("possibly.s1","通用","The missing key could possibly be in the coat pocket, although the search team has not checked it yet.","遺失的鑰匙也許在外套口袋裡，不過搜尋人員還沒檢查那裡。","Possibly is used to say that something might be true.","Possibly modifies could be; although marks the uncertainty and missing check.","The location is suggested as a chance, not stated as certain.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The package could possibly have arrived, meaning it was certainly delivered and no one needed to check.","The package could possibly have arrived, but its delivery still needed confirmation.","Possibly marks uncertainty or chance, not certainty that the event happened.")],quiz:[Q("possibly.q1","possibly.s1","meaning","If someone says it could possibly rain, they mean rain ___.",["might happen","is impossible","has already ended"],"might happen","Possibly expresses a chance rather than certainty.","meaning"),Q("possibly.q2","possibly.s1","context","Which sentence uses possibly to show uncertainty?",["The train could possibly arrive early, but the schedule is not confirmed.","The train certainly arrived yesterday.","The station is made of porcelain."],"The train could possibly arrive early, but the schedule is not confirmed.","The speaker presents early arrival as a chance.","context"),Q("possibly.q3","possibly.s1","word-choice","The package may ____ reach the island before the storm.",["possibly","possessor","pompous"],"possibly","The adverb marks the package’s uncertain chance of arriving.","word-choice"),Q("possibly.q4","possibly.s1","meaning","Why add possibly to “We can finish today”?",["It signals that finishing is a chance, not a guarantee.","It means the work is already complete.","It changes the sentence into a question about ownership."],"It signals that finishing is a chance, not a guarantee.","Possibly makes the statement less certain.","meaning")]});
